# Unity Project Structure

This project will scale a lot. Keep the structure clean, predictable, and feature-first.

## Target layout

```
<ProjectRoot>/
  Assets/
    Scripts/{Feature}/        # runtime code, grouped by feature
    Editor/{Feature}/         # editor-only code, mirrors Scripts/{Feature}
    Prefabs/{Feature}/
    Scenes/
    Materials/{Feature}/
    ScriptableObjects/{Feature}/
    Art/{Models|Textures|Animations}/{Feature}/
    Audio/{Music|SFX}/
    Settings/                 # render pipeline, input, volume profiles, etc.
    Tests/EditMode/
    Tests/PlayMode/
    Plugins/                  # third-party only - never edit
  Docs/
    ChangeSummaries/          # lives OUTSIDE Assets on purpose
  Packages/
  ProjectSettings/
```

Examples of `{Feature}`: `Core`, `Environment`, `Gameplay`, `Player`, `UI`, `Audio`, `Utilities`.

## Rules
- Feature-first: the same `{Feature}` name is reused across `Scripts`, `Editor`, `Prefabs`, `Materials`, etc.
- Never put files directly in `Assets/` root or directly in `Assets/Scripts/` (always use a feature subfolder).
- Before creating a file, LOOK at the existing structure and reuse a matching feature folder.
  If a new feature folder is needed, create it and mention it in the change summary.
- Keep nesting shallow (max ~4 levels under `Assets/Scripts/`). Split by feature, not by type (no giant `Managers/` or `Helpers/` dumping grounds).
- One top-level type per file. The filename must equal the class name.
- Folders and files: PascalCase, no spaces, no special characters.
- Unity-reserved folder names must be spelled exactly: `Assets`, `Editor`, `Resources`, `StreamingAssets`, `Plugins`, `Gizmos`.
  Avoid `Resources/` unless there is a strong reason (it bloats builds and hides dependencies).
- Editor-only code ALWAYS goes under `Assets/Editor/{Feature}/`. Never place editor code in runtime folders
  and never reference `UnityEditor` from runtime scripts (unless wrapped in `#if UNITY_EDITOR`).

## Namespaces
- Runtime: `MyGame.{Feature}` (e.g. `MyGame.Environment`)
- Editor: `MyGame.{Feature}.Editor` (e.g. `MyGame.Environment.Editor`)
- Replace `MyGame` with the real root namespace. Detect it from existing scripts; if there is none, ask once.

## Assembly definitions
- Do NOT create `.asmdef` files unless the project already uses them or the user asks.
- If asmdefs exist: an Editor script needs its own Editor asmdef (include platform: Editor only)
  that references the runtime asmdef of the same feature.
- Without asmdefs, Unity automatically compiles `Editor/` folders into the editor assembly.

## Before you finish any task
- Every new file is in the folder this document prescribes.
- No stray files in `Assets/` root. No empty placeholder folders unless the user asked for scaffolding.
